  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improving Flood and Drought-Related Preparedness Efforts in Disaster Affected Areas of Somalia</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Since the collapse of the Somali central government in 1991, Somalia has suffered a highly complex protracted conflict characterized by changing political dynamics and power shifts. Although there have been improvements to the peace and security situation in Somalia since the formation of the Federal Government of Somalia (FGS) in 2012, the overall situation remains fragile with Al-Shabaab (AS) being a major obstacle to peace and security. Forced displacement remains an ongoing reality for people living in Somalia due to conflict, environmental hardships, prolonged droughts and flash floods, combined with inadequate resource distributions that are often exacerbated by clan-based, ideological differences and land and natural resources competition. With the main objective to contribute to improved resilience for vulnerable communities affected by recurrent disasters including droughts and floods, IOM proposes an integrated intervention to strengthen Community Based Disaster Risk Reduction Capacity and building the capacity of national institutions. The intervention focuses on Gedo and Lower Juba regions, but is also ensuring support to coordination mechanisms at national level to benefit all.</narrative>
  </description>
